<edge source="eurozone_bond_stress::market_volatility" target="china_taiwan_grayzone::investor_risk_premium" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Spikes in eurozone market volatility increase global hedge and margin costs and push up cross‑asset implied volatilities, which lifts the cost of insuring and holding assets linked to Taiwan and therefore raises the investor risk premium on those exposures.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">eurozone_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">china_taiwan_grayzone</data>
</edge>
<edge source="eurozone_bond_stress::market_volatility" target="iran_houthi_maritime::shipping_risk_premium" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Spikes in eurozone-driven financial market volatility raise cross‑asset risk premia and push insurers, owners and charterers to reprice hazard exposure for Red Sea/GoG transits, lifting the shipping risk premium demanded on tankers.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">eurozone_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_houthi_maritime</data>
</edge>
<edge source="eurozone_bond_stress::market_volatility" target="russia_ukraine_war::diesel_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Increased eurozone fixed‑income and related market volatility raises risk premia and drives short‑term flows into commodity futures and physical commodity hoarding, pushing up diesel spot and futures prices across major hubs.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">eurozone_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
