<edge source="eurozone_bond_stress::cross_border_flows" target="china_taiwan_grayzone::investor_risk_premium" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">An abrupt net outflow of capital from eurozone sovereign and credit markets triggers global portfolio rebalancing and liquidity withdrawal from Asia, reducing demand for Taiwan‑exposed assets and raising the risk premium investors require to hold them.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">eurozone_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">china_taiwan_grayzone</data>
</edge>
<edge source="eurozone_bond_stress::cross_border_flows" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_evasion_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher net cross‑border capital flows through eurozone channels increase the availability of financing for shipping, insurance and opaque intermediary firms that facilitate rerouting/payments around sanctions, raising the rate of sanctions evasion.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">eurozone_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="eurozone_bond_stress::cross_border_flows" target="us_china_ai_rivalry::compute_supply_capacity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">An increase in net cross‑border capital into eurozone sovereign/credit markets diverts institutional capital away from data‑centre and hyperscaler expansion elsewhere, reducing the pace of compute capacity buildouts in US/China, whereas a decline in those flows tends to redirect investment toward US/Chinese cloud expansion.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">eurozone_bond_stress</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_ai_rivalry</data>
</edge>
